##
    Asking a person something.

    Console.Read gives back what they typed, or nothing at all when there is nothing left to
    read — the end of a file piped in, or a person pressing Ctrl+D. That is why it yields an
    optional: "they typed nothing more" is an answer, not a fault.

    Everything they type arrives as text, so anything else has to be read out of it. Every one
    of those readings yields an optional too, for the same reason: text that will not read is
    the ordinary case when a person is doing the typing.

    Run it with something piped in:

        echo 7 | pc run samples/asking.pc
##

global model Program

    function Main()
        Program.AskingForAName();
        Program.AskingForANumber();
        Program.WhenNothingComes();
    end function

    # ---- Text, which needs no reading ---------------------------------------------------

    function AskingForAName()
        Console.Write("your name? ");

        string? typed = Console.Read();

        if typed.HasValue()
            Console.WriteLine("hello, {{typed.Value()}}");
        else
            Console.WriteLine("nobody there");
        end if
    end function

    # ---- A number, which does ------------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        Two questions, and both must be asked. Was anything typed? And did what they typed
        read as a number? Neither can be skipped, and the compiler will not let them be: an
        optional cannot be used until its presence is proven.
    ##
    function AskingForANumber()
        Console.Write("a number? ");

        string? typed = Console.Read();

        if not typed.HasValue()
            Console.WriteLine("nothing to read");
            yield;
        end if

        integer? number = typed.Value().Trim().ToInteger();

        if number.HasValue()
            Console.WriteLine("{{number.Value()}} doubled is {{number.Value() * 2}}");
        else
            Console.WriteLine("'{{typed.Value()}}' is not a number");
        end if
    end function

    # ---- Or say what to do instead --------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        Or gives a value to fall back on, which turns both questions into one line when a
        sensible default exists. What it must not do is hide a mistake worth reporting — here
        an unreadable answer really does mean "take none", so it is the right shape.
    ##
    function WhenNothingComes()
        Console.Write("how many? ");

        integer count = Console.Read().Or("0").Trim().ToInteger().Or(0);

        Console.WriteLine("taking {{count}}");
    end function

end model
